BT
/F2 11 Tf 56 800 Td 14 TL
/F2 14 Tf (Mendel's pea plant experiments) Tj 0 -22 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(Gregor Mendel, a monk who worked in the 1800s, studied inheritance by crossing pea plants) Tj T*
(that showed contrasting traits, such as tall and short stems.) Tj T*
T*
(When Mendel crossed pure-breeding tall plants with pure-breeding short plants, all of the) Tj T*
(offspring in the first generation, called F1, were tall. When he let the F1 plants) Tj T*
(self-pollinate, about three-fourths of the second generation, called F2, were tall and about) Tj T*
(one-fourth were short.) Tj T*
T*
(From these results Mendel concluded that each plant carries two factors for a trait, which) Tj T*
(we now call alleles. One allele can hide the effect of the other: the allele that shows is) Tj T*
(dominant and the hidden one is recessive. The two alleles separate when sex cells \(gametes\)) Tj T*
(are formed, so each gamete carries only one allele. This idea is called the law of) Tj T*
(segregation.) Tj T*
T*
ET
endstream
