BT
/F2 11 Tf 56 800 Td 14 TL
/F2 14 Tf (Using a Punnett square) Tj 0 -22 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(Geneticists write the dominant allele with a capital letter and the recessive allele with) Tj T*
(the same letter in lowercase. For stem length, T stands for tall and t stands for short.) Tj T*
T*
(The combination of alleles an organism has is its genotype. The trait that shows is its) Tj T*
(phenotype. An organism with two identical alleles, TT or tt, is homozygous. An organism with) Tj T*
(two different alleles, Tt, is heterozygous. A heterozygous pea plant is tall, because T is) Tj T*
(dominant.) Tj T*
T*
(A Punnett square shows all the possible allele combinations from a cross. In a cross between) Tj T*
(two heterozygous plants \(Tt x Tt\), the offspring genotypes are TT, Tt, Tt and tt. The) Tj T*
(genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, and the phenotypic ratio is 3 tall : 1 short. The) Tj T*
(probability that any one offspring is short is 1 in 4, or 25 percent.) Tj T*
T*
ET
endstream
